From: android-kitkat-tls12 <android-kitkat-tls12@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 31/32] HttpsURLConnection: fall back to TLSv1 with
 TLS_FALLBACK_SCSV, not SSLv3

Jelly Bean equivalent of external_okhttp/0001 (okhttp c3f6560, "SCSV
support") and external_okhttp/0002 (TLSv1, not SSLv3, for the retry)
of the 4.4 series. Android 4.1-4.3 have no okhttp: HttpsURLConnection
is libcore.net.http, and its retry after a failed handshake is the
tlsTolerant=false branch of HttpConnection.setupSecureSocket(), which
enabled {"SSLv3"} only. With this series SSLv3 is supported but no
longer enabled by default (0006), and a server that still speaks it is
open to POODLE.

The retry now enables {"TLSv1"} only and appends TLS_FALLBACK_SCSV when
the socket lists it in getSupportedCipherSuites() (the code of okhttp's
Platform.supportTlsIntolerantServer() after 0001 + 0002, as a private
helper of HttpConnection). A server whose highest version is TLSv1
accepts the retry; one that supports TLSv1.1 or TLSv1.2 rejects it with
the inappropriate_fallback alert, so a middlebox cannot force the
downgrade.

How Jelly Bean differs: stock 4.2 also took the fallback branch on the
first attempt for any socket that is not an OpenSSLSocketImpl (a socket
from an app's own provider, or an app's wrapping SSLSocket). With the
fallback now sending TLSv1 + SCSV, such a socket would be refused by
every TLSv1.2 server on both attempts. The first attempt therefore
keeps the factory's default protocols for every socket and sets session
tickets and the host name only on an OpenSSLSocketImpl, as 4.4 okhttp
does (Connection.upgradeToTls / Platform.enableTlsExtensions). The retry
stays without session tickets and setHostname(), as before; SNI still
goes out from the host the socket was created with (0013).

Tests: URLConnectionTest testSslFallback,
testSslFallback_rejectedByTls12Server and
testSslFallback_socketWithoutScsvSupport (0021, 0030), which failed on
stock 4.2; testSslFallback_socketWithoutScsvSupport also checks that the
first attempt of a non-OpenSSL socket keeps the default protocols.

Android 4.2 port (android-4.2.2_r1) of patch 0031 of the Android 4.3
series (no 4.4 libcore counterpart: 4.4 has this change in
external_okhttp 0001 and 0002). libcore.net.http.HttpConnection and
HttpsURLConnectionImpl are the same on 4.2 and 4.3; only line offsets
differ, and the test comment names stock 4.2.
